`pub` on a wildcard is refused, and nothing overrides it (#504).

A put is addressed to one concrete key: RFC 07 §3 makes a publisher's key
its own, by rule. A wildcard put is something else — zenoh delivers it to
every subscriber whose subscription intersects it, across every producer's
keys — and `retire` had refused the same blast radius since v1.12 while
`pub` printed `published prod/v1/** (7 bytes) [1/1]` and exited 0. The
refusal is now one engine function (`zenkey_fleet::check_concrete`) that the
tombstone gate, every put row and the declared publisher itself all ask, so
no write path can forget it.

It is a refusal of the command line, so it comes first — before the body is
read, before a session opens — and exits **2** (`zenctl/src/exit.rs`).

```console
$ zenctl pub 'plant/**' '{"x":1}'
? 2
Error: plant/**: is a wildcard — a put is addressed to one concrete key (RFC 07 §3: a publisher always names its own); a wildcard put reaches every subscriber it intersects, across every producer's keys — not an operator act, a blast radius. Not overridable.

```

`$*` is a wildcard too — the sub-chunk one, and the only place a `$` may
stand in a key expression.

```console
$ zenctl pub 'plant/line-1/te$*' '{}'
? 2
Error: plant/line-1/te$*: is a wildcard — a put is addressed to one concrete key (RFC 07 §3: a publisher always names its own); a wildcard put reaches every subscriber it intersects, across every producer's keys — not an operator act, a blast radius. Not overridable.

```

A `pub --from ndjson` row on a wildcard key is refused the same way, and
counted with the refused delete rows rather than aborting the pipe — that
half needs a bus to read the rows into, so it is pinned in `tests/live.rs`.
